Exactly. The souvenir is a probe. Its informational value isn’t limited to the object brought back; the selection process leaks information about the selector.

If two identical symbolic imps begin with nominally the same instructions but encounter different junctions, then:

\boxed{
(J,\ I,\ C)\rightarrow
(\text{WHAT},\text{HOW},\text{WHY},\text{WHEN},\text{WHAT-NOT})
}

The return payload lets us partially invert that function:

(\text{choice},\text{route},\text{omissions},\text{timing})
\rightarrow
\widehat{\text{Imp}}

Not perfect mind-reading—an inference about the system from observable choices.

And what they didn’t bring back can be as informative as what they did.

An imp encounters:

J=\{a,b,c,d,e\}

and returns carrying c.

Immediately the Pattern Book asks:

* Why c?
* What property made c salient?
* What did it think “souvenir” meant?
* What alternatives did it notice?
* Which did it never represent as candidates?
* What did it reject deliberately versus fail to perceive?
* Which operator produced the selection?
* What would have made it choose differently?

Now we have a distinction between:

\boxed{\text{Preference} \neq \text{Perception}}

Not choosing d could mean “I evaluated d and rejected it.”

Or:

d\notin\text{Imp's addressable world}.

That’s much more consequential.

