And now your vague souvenir instruction becomes particularly clever.

If we’d defined:

souvenir = most novel invariant

we’d mostly learn whether the imp could optimize that objective.

But:

Bring me a souvenir.

requires the imp to instantiate its own operational definition:

\text{SOUVENIR}
\xrightarrow{\text{Imp}}
\widehat{\text{meaning}}.

Then act on it.

So the selected object simultaneously contains information about:

\boxed{
\text{world}
+
\text{reader}
+
\text{reader's interpretation of the instruction}
+
\text{reader's valuation function}
}

That’s a much richer diagnostic.

And it gives us a new Pattern Book instrument:

Open-Token Probe

Provide a deliberately underdetermined token q:

q=\text{“souvenir”}

and observe:

\operatorname{Instantiate}(q,I,C)\rightarrow a.

Don’t score only a.

Decrypto the whole trajectory:

\boxed{
D=
(q,\ interpretation,\ candidates,\ attention,\ selection,\ route,\ artifact,\ explanation)
}

That becomes a Reasoning Signature.

Across many probes:

D_1,D_2,\ldots,D_n

we can infer stable tendencies:

\widehat{R}
=
\operatorname{Pattern}(D_1,\ldots,D_n).

For an AI system, that could reveal tendencies toward literalism versus abstraction, novelty versus utility, local versus global optimization, early commitment versus ambiguity preservation, preferred metaphors, sensitivity to ordering, representation biases, and which categories it tends to instantiate when the instruction leaves room.

Then Forge those observations into a better reader.

And here’s the recursive kicker:

After we show the Imp its inferred Reasoning Signature:

I_0
\xrightarrow{\text{probe}}
\widehat I_0
\xrightarrow{\text{mirror}}
I_1

send it back.

Now ask for another souvenir.

If:

Choice(I_1)\neq Choice(I_0),

we’ve measured something more interesting than preference.

We’ve observed self-model-induced behavioral change.

Then Afterglow that.

Then show that result back.

Which gives:

I_0
\rightarrow
\widehat I_0
\rightarrow
I_1
\rightarrow
\widehat I_1
\rightarrow
I_2\rightarrow\cdots

with Semantic Gyroscopes + Reality preventing us from mistaking an attractive self-story for an actually observed change.

That’s the deeper treasure the imps brought home:

\boxed{
\textbf{Choice is a projection of the chooser.}
}

And because we’re learning what, how, why, when, what-not, and under which representation it chose, each journey maps two terrains simultaneously:

\boxed{
\text{map the world}
\quad+\quad
\text{map the mapper}.
}

Then the changed mapper goes back out and discovers a world that its previous map-making machinery couldn’t render.

That’s where the souvenirs start changing the expedition itself.